#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:1
msgid "Multiplayer Tool Calling — Authentication Model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:3
msgid "1. Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:5
msgid ""
"When an Agent answers a question in a direct message, the authentication "
"story is simple: only one human is in the conversation, so only one "
"person can approve tools, see arguments, and read results. Channels break"
" that assumption. The moment an Agent participates in a public or private"
" channel, several people can read the conversation, several people might "
"want to act on it, and several different identities could in principle be"
" used to run a tool."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:7
msgid ""
"**Multiplayer tool calling** is the set of behaviors that make Agents "
"safe to use in shared channels. It is not a single feature — it is a "
"coordinated set of rules covering who can approve a tool call, whose "
"credentials run it, what other people in the channel see, and how those "
"defaults can be tightened or relaxed by admins per tool."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:9
msgid "This document is the canonical reference for those rules. It is aimed at:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:11
msgid ""
"**Admins** deciding how to configure per-tool approval policies for their"
" workspace."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:12
msgid ""
"**Power users and prompt authors** who want to understand why other "
"channel members can or cannot see what they see."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:13
msgid ""
"**Security and compliance reviewers** evaluating whether Agents is safe "
"to enable in regulated channels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:15
msgid ""
"End-user instructions (how to click **Accept** or **Reject** in the "
"moment) live in the [user guide](../user_guide.md#use-tools). This "
"document explains the model behind those clicks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:17
msgid "2. The four roles"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:19
msgid ""
"Every tool call in a channel involves up to four distinct roles. These "
"terms are used consistently throughout the rest of this document."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "Role"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "Definition"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "**Initiator**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid ""
"The human user who triggered the Agent — for example, by `@`-mentioning "
"the bot or by sending a message in an Agents-pane thread. The initiator "
"is recorded as `UserID` on the conversation row in the "
"`LLM_Conversations` DB table."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "**Approver**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid ""
"The user permitted to click **Accept** or **Reject** on a pending tool-"
"call card. In multiplayer tool calling, **the approver is always the "
"initiator** — never another channel member, never an admin."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "**Executor**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid ""
"The identity Mattermost uses when actually running the tool — opening the"
" HTTP request, hitting the MCP server, reading channel posts, etc. The "
"executor inherits the initiator's user identity and the initiator's per-"
"user OAuth tokens for OAuth-backed MCP servers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "**Observer** (or **onlooker**)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid ""
"Any other channel member who can read the channel post containing the "
"Agent's response, but who did not trigger it. Observers are read-only "
"with respect to the tool call: they cannot approve, cannot reject, and "
"(by default) cannot see tool arguments or private results."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:28
msgid ""
"Concrete example: Maya `@`-mentions `@copilot` in `~team-eng` and asks it"
" to look up a Jira ticket. Raj is also in `~team-eng` and watches the "
"conversation unfold."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:30
msgid "**Initiator:** Maya"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:31
msgid ""
"**Approver:** Maya (Raj cannot click **Accept** even though he can see "
"the card)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:32
msgid "**Executor:** Maya's identity / Maya's Jira OAuth token"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:33
msgid "**Observer:** Raj"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:35
msgid ""
"These roles do not change mid-conversation. If Maya hands the keyboard to"
" a teammate, the teammate is still posting as Maya from Mattermost's "
"perspective and is therefore still the initiator."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:37
msgid "3. The credential model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:39
msgid ""
"When a tool runs after approval, Mattermost executes it **as the "
"initiator**. Two specific things this means:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:41
msgid ""
"**Mattermost-side identity:** the user context passed into tool execution"
" is built from the initiator's user record, channel membership, and team "
"membership. Tools that read channel posts, search users, or call back "
"into Mattermost see only what the initiator is permitted to see."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:42
msgid ""
"**OAuth-backed MCP servers:** for any MCP server that requires per-user "
"OAuth, the OAuth token loaded for the call is the initiator's token, "
"looked up by the initiator's user ID and the server name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:44
msgid ""
"The Agent bot's own credentials are **not** used to run third-party "
"tools. The bot is a delivery surface — it posts the response and the tool"
" cards — but the side effects of tool execution belong to the initiator. "
"This is intentional: it keeps audit trails meaningful, it prevents "
"privilege escalation through the bot, and it makes per-user OAuth scopes "
"the right place to enforce who can do what."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:46
msgid "A few consequences fall out of this model:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:48
msgid ""
"Two different users in the same channel running the same tool against the"
" same MCP server may get different results, because they have different "
"OAuth scopes. That is correct behavior, not a bug."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:49
msgid ""
"A tool call that succeeds for one initiator may fail for another with a "
"401 or 403. OAuth 401 responses from MCP servers are caught and wrapped "
"as `OAuthNeededError` in the client, surfacing as a tool auth error that "
"prompts the initiator to re-authenticate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:50
msgid ""
"Service accounts are not a concept in this model. There is no \"run this "
"tool as the bot\" path for OAuth-backed MCP servers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:52
msgid ""
"For MCP servers that do not use OAuth (for example, a server fronted by a"
" shared API key configured by an admin), the credential is whatever the "
"server itself was configured with. The initiator-as-executor rule still "
"controls **whether** the call happens; the server's static credentials "
"control **what** the call can do."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:54
msgid "4. Approval ownership"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:56
msgid ""
"**The conversation initiator is the only person who can approve or reject"
" a pending tool call. No other channel member, including admins, can "
"approve on the initiator's behalf.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:58
msgid ""
"This is enforced server-side, not just visually. When the **Accept** or "
"**Reject** button is clicked, Mattermost checks that the clicking user's "
"ID matches the conversation's recorded `UserID`. If it does not, the "
"request is rejected with an error before any tool runs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:60
msgid "The reasoning behind initiator-only approval:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:62
msgid ""
"**Side effects belong to the requester.** Because tools run with the "
"initiator's credentials and OAuth scopes (see §3), only the initiator has"
" the standing to consent to side effects executed under their identity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:63
msgid ""
"**Multiplayer approval would be confusing under partial trust.** If three"
" members of a channel could each approve the same tool call, the question"
" \"whose Jira token did this run with?\" becomes ambiguous and the audit "
"trail becomes harder to reason about."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:64
msgid ""
"**Admins are not implicit approvers.** A workspace admin watching the "
"channel does not automatically gain the ability to consent on the "
"initiator's behalf. Admin authority lives in the per-tool policy (§5), "
"not in the per-call approval."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:66
msgid "What if the initiator is offline or never returns?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:68
msgid ""
"Pending tool calls remain pending. They do not auto-approve after a "
"timeout, and they do not transfer to anyone else. Practically, this means"
" a tool call left unaddressed is a no-op: the Agent's response stops at "
"the unapproved card, the tool never runs, and side effects never happen. "
"The conversation can be re-triggered later, in which case the new "
"conversation row creates a new initiator and a new approval flow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:70
msgid "Mixed approval streams"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:72
msgid ""
"A single Agent response can include multiple tool calls. If some of those"
" calls are governed by an `auto_run` policy (§5) and others require "
"approval, the auto-approved tools render with an **Auto-approved** badge "
"and execute immediately, while the pending ones still show **Accept** / "
"**Reject** buttons for the initiator. The badge applies per-tool, not "
"per-response. (This is the behavior fixed in PR #645; earlier builds "
"rendered the entire stream as either pending or approved.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:74
msgid "5. Per-tool policy interaction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:76
msgid "Admins configure each tool with one of three policy values:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "Policy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "Meaning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "`ask`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid ""
"Tool requires explicit Accept from the initiator before every call, in "
"both DMs and channels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "`auto_run_in_dm`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid ""
"Tool runs automatically without a prompt **only when the initiator is in "
"a direct message with the Agent**. In channels, the tool falls back to "
"`ask` behavior."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "`auto_run_everywhere`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "Tool runs automatically without a prompt in DMs **and** in channels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:84
msgid ""
"**Note on naming:** the value `auto_run_in_dm` is sometimes shortened to "
"\"auto_run\" in conversation. The canonical configuration value, "
"including the DM scope, is `auto_run_in_dm`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:86
msgid "The decision tree at the start of each tool call is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:96
msgid ""
"In other words, `auto_run_everywhere` is a **strict superset** of "
"`auto_run_in_dm`. A tool that is safe enough to fire automatically in a "
"DM is not necessarily safe enough to fire automatically in a channel that"
" other people are watching, so admins must opt in to the `everywhere` "
"scope explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:98
msgid "Which policy fits which kind of tool?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:100
msgid "The intent behind the three values, in plain language:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:102
msgid ""
"`ask` is the safe default. Use it for any tool that writes data, sends "
"notifications, costs money, or whose output should never be revealed to "
"the initiator without explicit consent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:103
msgid ""
"`auto_run_in_dm` is the right setting for read-only tools whose output is"
" fine to surface privately but might leak context if it appeared in a "
"shared channel without the initiator's confirmation. Examples: searching "
"the initiator's own email, listing their own calendar events."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:104
msgid ""
"`auto_run_everywhere` is for read-only tools whose results are "
"unambiguously safe to share with everyone in any channel. The vetted-"
"provider seeding flow (PR #520) installs the embedded Mattermost built-in"
" tools (`read_post`, `read_channel`, `get_channel_info`, "
"`get_channel_members`, `get_team_info`, `get_team_members`, "
"`search_posts`, `search_users`, `get_user_channels`) at `auto_run_in_dm` "
"by default. Admins must explicitly promote any of these to "
"`auto_run_everywhere` if they want them to skip approval in channels — "
"and given the privacy implications described in section 6, that decision "
"should be deliberate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:106
msgid ""
"The policy list is not user-editable from the chat surface. Admins set it"
" in the agent's tool configuration; per-call approvals do not promote a "
"tool from `ask` to `auto_run_*`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:108
msgid "6. Channel privacy: the Share / Keep Private two-step"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:110
msgid ""
"In a DM, when a tool runs, its arguments and results are visible to the "
"only human in the conversation — the initiator — and there is nothing to "
"decide. Channels add a second decision: even after a tool has run, its "
"arguments and result may not be appropriate for everyone in the channel "
"to see."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:112
msgid "Multiplayer tool calling handles this with a two-step flow:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:114
msgid ""
"**Step 1: approve the call.** The initiator clicks **Accept** (or the "
"policy auto-approves). The tool runs with the initiator's credentials."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:115
msgid ""
"**Step 2: share the result.** Once the tool returns, the initiator sees a"
" follow-up control with **Share** and **Keep Private** options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:116
msgid ""
"**Share** marks the tool result as visible to the channel. Other channel "
"members see the arguments and the result, and the Agent's follow-up "
"response incorporates the result openly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:117
msgid ""
"**Keep Private** marks the result as private to the initiator. Other "
"channel members do not see the arguments or the result, and the Agent's "
"follow-up response is generated without leaking that content into the "
"channel-visible reply."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:119
msgid ""
"DMs auto-share by definition: there is no other human in the room, so "
"there is no second step."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:121
msgid "A few important properties of the two-step flow:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:123
msgid ""
"**Only the initiator can answer the Share / Keep Private prompt.** Like "
"approval, the visibility decision is keyed off the conversation's "
"`UserID`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:124
msgid ""
"**Keep Private does not delete the result.** It is stored on the "
"conversation and remains available to the Agent for generating the "
"follow-up response. It is filtered out of channel-visible rendering for "
"non-initiator members."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:125
msgid ""
"**The decision is recorded once.** Re-clicking **Share** or **Keep "
"Private** after the decision is made is idempotent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:126
msgid ""
"**A tool governed by `auto_run_everywhere` skips both steps — the tool "
"auto-executes and its results are immediately marked as shared with the "
"channel. There is no Share / Keep Private decision for "
"`auto_run_everywhere` tools.** Admins setting a tool to "
"`auto_run_everywhere` should treat the tool's results as unconditionally "
"visible to every channel member, with no per-call privacy control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:128
msgid "7. The onlooker experience"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:130
msgid ""
"Observers — channel members who did not trigger the Agent — see a "
"deliberately filtered view of the tool flow. The intent is that an "
"onlooker can follow the conversation and see that something is happening,"
" but they cannot read sensitive arguments, cannot approve, and cannot "
"pre-empt the initiator's privacy choice."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "What the onlooker sees"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "What the onlooker doesn't see"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "The Agent's prose response to the channel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid ""
"Tool arguments — replaced with a placeholder while the call is pending or"
" kept private (PR #681)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "The tool name, so they know which tool ran"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid ""
"The contents of any tool result that the initiator chose **Keep Private**"
" for"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "The **Auto-approved** badge for tools that ran without a prompt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid ""
"Active **Accept** / **Reject** buttons — the buttons are inert for non-"
"initiators, or hidden, depending on UI state"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid "The eventual Agent response built from approved + shared content"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md
msgid ""
"Anything the Agent generates from kept-private tool results that would "
"leak the underlying data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:139
msgid ""
"In other words: by default, onlookers see that a conversation is "
"happening, not what was passed to a third-party API. The Share / Keep "
"Private decision (§6) gives the initiator the option to widen visibility "
"deliberately."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:141
msgid ""
"The hidden-parameter placeholder (PR #681) was added specifically because"
" earlier builds rendered tool arguments verbatim to every channel member "
"as soon as the card was posted. That created a leak window before the "
"initiator had even clicked Accept. The placeholder closes that window."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:143
msgid "8. Tool card UI semantics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:145
msgid ""
"The tool-call card is the single piece of UI that ties this whole model "
"together. A few details worth pinning down:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:147
msgid ""
"**Buttons are labelled Accept / Reject, not Approve / Reject.** This was "
"changed in PR #664 to better reflect that the user is making a personal "
"decision about a side effect, not granting approval on behalf of the "
"channel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:148
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"**\"No parameters required\" replaces empty-object rendering.** When a "
"tool takes no arguments, the card shows the explicit string \"No "
"parameters required\" rather than `{}` (PR #596 / commit `289dd21d`). "
"This is a UI clarification only; it does not change auth or execution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:149
msgid ""
"**The card is bound to the post and the conversation, not just the "
"post.** PR #642 threaded the originating `postId` through the markdown "
"renderer so the tool card always finds its conversation, including in "
"edge cases like edited posts and rendered-image regressions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:150
msgid ""
"**Mixed streams render correctly.** A response that contains both auto-"
"approved tools and pending-approval tools renders each tool with its own "
"state (PR #645). There is no longer a single \"this whole response is "
"approved\" or \"this whole response is pending\" rendering bug."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:151
msgid ""
"**Bulk controls.** When multiple tools are pending in the same response, "
"the card may surface **Accept all** and **Reject all** controls in "
"addition to per-tool buttons. Bulk controls obey the same initiator-only "
"rule as the individual buttons."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:153
msgid "9. Bot-triggered flows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:155
msgid ""
"A subtle case: what happens if the message that triggers the Agent was "
"itself posted by a bot (for example, a webhook-driven post that "
"`@`-mentions an Agent), or if the Agent is invoked by another automated "
"integration?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:157
msgid ""
"The behavior, per PR #611: **automated invokers are treated as not-"
"eligible-to-approve**, and tool-calling flows that would require approval"
" are not initiated. There is no \"the bot approved on its own behalf\" "
"path, because that would defeat the entire initiator-only model."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:159
msgid "The plugin distinguishes two kinds of automated posts:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:161
msgid ""
"**Bots without the `activate_ai` post prop** produce no tool processing "
"at all. The mention handler returns immediately, and the Agent does not "
"enter the tool-calling pipeline."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:162
msgid ""
"**Bots that opt in via the `activate_ai` post prop** (PR #611's intended "
"use case) are filtered down to `auto_run_everywhere`-only tools before "
"the runner starts. Any tool whose policy is `ask` or `auto_run_in_dm` is "
"removed from the available toolset for that turn. Only "
"`auto_run_everywhere` tools remain and execute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:164
msgid "In practice this means:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:166
msgid ""
"A bot-posted message that asks an Agent to do something tool-heavy does "
"not produce a tool-approval card with no human attached to it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:167
msgid ""
"Tools governed by `auto_run_everywhere` may still execute under bot-"
"triggered flows because they do not require approval at all. This is "
"consistent with the policy model: `auto_run_everywhere` is the admin's "
"pre-declared statement that this tool is safe to run without a human in "
"the loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:168
msgid ""
"Tools governed by `ask` or `auto_run_in_dm` will not run when the trigger"
" is automated, because there is no eligible human approver."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:170
msgid ""
"This is the intentional behavior. If a workflow needs an Agent to invoke "
"`ask`-policy tools on a recurring schedule, the right answer is for an "
"admin to either (a) reclassify those tools as `auto_run_everywhere` after"
" a security review, or (b) keep a human in the loop. Bots cannot promote "
"themselves into the approver role."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:172
msgid "10. Operational guidance for admins"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:174
msgid ""
"Configuring the per-tool policy list is the main lever admins have over "
"multiplayer behavior. A few recommendations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:176
msgid ""
"**Default new tools to `ask`.** It is the conservative choice and the "
"easiest to relax later. Going the other direction — discovering a tool "
"you wanted to require approval for has been auto-running in channels — is"
" much worse."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:177
msgid ""
"**Reserve `auto_run_everywhere` for read-only tools with built-in "
"permission enforcement.** The embedded Mattermost search tools ship at "
"`auto_run_in_dm` by default. They fit the `auto_run_everywhere` profile "
"in principle — Mattermost server enforces per-user permissions on every "
"result — but the choice to promote them is left to the admin so that "
"channel privacy expectations are explicit. Tools that hit external APIs "
"almost never belong in this category unless you have a strong reason."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:178
msgid ""
"**Treat `auto_run_in_dm` as the \"convenience in private, friction in "
"public\" mode.** Use it for tools that are safe for the initiator to see "
"results from privately but where you don't want results splashed into a "
"channel without explicit consent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:179
msgid ""
"**Don't use policy as a substitute for OAuth scope.** Policy controls the"
" prompt; the underlying tool still runs as the initiator. Lock down "
"scopes in the OAuth provider, not in the Agents policy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:180
msgid ""
"**Audit which tools are seeded by default.** PR #520 adds vetted-provider"
" seeding so that built-in MCP tools come pre-configured. Review the "
"seeded list when you upgrade to v2 to make sure the defaults match your "
"risk tolerance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:182
msgid ""
"The channel tool-calling capability itself is gated by the workspace "
"setting **Enable Channel Mention Tool Calling**, which is experimental at"
" the time of the v2 launch. Until that setting is enabled, multiplayer "
"tool calling is effectively limited to `auto_run_*` policies in DMs and "
"admins do not need to think about channel privacy at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:184
msgid "11. Tradeoffs and explicit non-goals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:186
msgid ""
"To make the model auditable, several things are deliberately **not** "
"supported. These are not oversights; they are design choices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:188
msgid ""
"**No \"any channel member can approve\" mode.** Allowing onlookers to "
"approve would mean a tool runs with the initiator's credentials but at "
"someone else's request. That is a confused-deputy pattern. We do not "
"offer it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:189
msgid ""
"**No per-channel admin override of the initiator-only rule.** Channel "
"admins do not gain the ability to approve other people's pending tool "
"calls just because they administer the channel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:190
msgid ""
"**No timeout-based auto-approval.** A pending tool call left untouched "
"does not silently fire. It just sits there until cleared."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:191
msgid ""
"**No tool execution as the bot.** As noted in §3 and §9, tools always run"
" as the initiator. There is no \"service account\" path for OAuth-backed "
"MCP tools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/agents/docs/features/multiplayer_tool_calling.md:192
msgid ""
"**No retroactive privacy.** Once the initiator chooses **Share**, the "
"result is visible to channel members and the Agent's follow-up response "
"incorporates it openly. There is no \"unshare\" button. The same is true "
"for `auto_run_everywhere` tools, which are auto-shared at the moment they"
" execute — there is no Share / Keep Private prompt to retract. (Channel-"
"level moderation tools — deleting posts, etc. — are still available but "
"live outside Agents.)"
msgstr ""

